@* An example is the rendered component and the source that produced it, on two tabs.

   The source is READ FROM THE FILE that was just rendered, not retyped beside it. Docs that carry
   a copy of their own code start lying the first time someone edits one half, and this library
   exists partly to make that impossible — it would be a poor showing to reintroduce it here.

   Every part of this is a component from the library: Tabs, Card and CodeBlock. That is not
   decoration. A component library whose own documentation is built out of hand-rolled markup has
   not shown that the components are enough to build anything — it has shown the opposite. *@

<Tabs Value="preview" Class="mt-4">
    <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-4">
        <TabsList>
            <TabsTrigger Value="preview">Preview</TabsTrigger>
            <TabsTrigger Value="code">Code</TabsTrigger>
        </TabsList>
        <span class="font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">@Source</span>
    </div>

    <TabsContent Value="preview">
        @* A plain dashed frame, not a Card: samples that need a Card render their own, and a
           nested Card is the most common visible defect in component documentation. *@
        <div class="flex min-h-[80px] flex-wrap items-start justify-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-dashed bg-card/30 px-6 py-8">
            @ChildContent
        </div>

        @* Where a sample's own request lands, and it has an ID.

           It used to be `class="preview"` on every example, which meant eleven identical
           targets on a page. Unpoly resolved `.preview` to the FIRST one in the document, so a
           form near the bottom answered into a card near the top — and swapping a fragment that
           far from the form left its [up-disable] bookkeeping pointing at replaced elements,
           which threw inside Unpoly on the next submit. One name, one element. *@
        <div id="@PreviewId" class="preview"></div>

    </TabsContent>

    <TabsContent Value="code" Hidden>
        <CodeBlock Code="@SourceText" Language="razor" Title="@Source" />
    </TabsContent>
</Tabs>

@code {
    /// <summary>File name of the sample, e.g. "ButtonVariants.razor".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Source { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// The id of this example's answer slot, derived from the sample's own file name so a
    /// sample can name it without anything having to agree twice: ButtonSpinner.razor targets
    /// #preview-button-spinner.
    /// </summary>
    string PreviewId => "preview-" + string.Concat(
        Path.GetFileNameWithoutExtension(Source)
            .Select((c, i) => char.IsUpper(c) && i > 0 ? "-" + char.ToLowerInvariant(c)
                                                       : char.ToLowerInvariant(c).ToString()));

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    string SourceText = "";

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        var path = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "samples", Source);
        SourceText = File.Exists(path)
            ? Trim(File.ReadAllText(path))
            : $"(sample not found: samples/{Source} — is it under Components/Examples?)";
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Drops the leading `@inherits`/`@using` lines and the trailing @@code block: a reader wants
    /// the markup, and the plumbing above it is noise they would skip past every time.
    /// </summary>
    static string Trim(string text)
    {
        var lines = text.Replace("\r\n", "\n").Split('\n').ToList();
        // A Razor comment spans lines, and only its first one starts with '@'. Dropping lines by
        // that test alone beheaded the comment and left the rest of it — and a dangling *@ — at
        // the top of every sample, which is what the samples had been showing.
        while (lines.Count > 0)
        {
            if (lines[0].Trim().Length == 0) { lines.RemoveAt(0); continue; }

            if (lines[0].TrimStart().StartsWith("@*"))
            {
                var end = lines.FindIndex(l => l.Contains("*@"));
                if (end < 0) break;
                lines.RemoveRange(0, end + 1);
                continue;
            }

            if (lines[0].StartsWith('@') && !lines[0].StartsWith("@code")) { lines.RemoveAt(0); continue; }
            break;
        }

        var code = lines.FindIndex(l => l.StartsWith("@code"));
        if (code >= 0) lines = lines[..code];

        return string.Join('\n', lines).Trim();
    }
}
